        en: "START WITH THE SIZE, BECAUSE EVERY CLINICAL CONSEQUENCE IN THIS SECTION FOLLOWS FROM HOW SMALL THIS GENOME IS. “ Mitochondria contain their own genome, a circular, double-stranded DNA (mtDNA) molecule ” , and the copy number is layered: each of the hundreds or thousands of mitochondria in a typical cell “ has about five copies of this genome ” . The human mitochondrial chromosome holds 37 genes in 16,569 bp, “ including 13 that encode subunits of proteins of the respiratory chain ” ; “ the remaining genes code for rRNA and tRNA molecules essential ” to a protein-synthesising machinery that belongs to the organelle alone — to build those 13 subunits “ mitochondria have their own ribosomes, distinctly different from those in the cytoplasm ” . Set against that, roughly 1,200 different mitochondrial proteins “ are encoded by nuclear genes, synthesized on cytoplasmic ribosomes ” and then imported (A p.692). Hold the ratio 13 : 1,200 in mind for the rest of the section: it is the reason a defect of oxidative phosphorylation is more often a nuclear problem than a mitochondrial one, and the reason a family history that looks Mendelian does not rule out a mitochondrial disease.",
